            {
              "id": "c9b4075f-1d43-33a1-ae33-214abac43734",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure or unsafe C functions in C++ programming. In this case, the function `alloca()` is used, which is considered unsafe. The `alloca()` function dynamically allocates memory on the stack, which is automatically freed when the function that called `alloca()` returns. This can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflow, stack overflow, or other memory corruption issues, which can lead to crashes, incorrect program behavior, or even code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `alloca()` function. Instead, use safer alternatives such as `malloc()`, `calloc()`, or `new` in C++. These functions allocate memory on the heap instead of the stack, which is safer. However, remember to manually free the memory allocated with `malloc()` or `calloc()` using `free()`, and the memory allocated with `new` using `delete`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `alloca()` function with `malloc()`, `calloc()`, or `new`. Here is an example of how to fix the code:\n\n```cpp\n// Old code\n// versionInfo = (char *) alloca(size);\n\n// New code\nversionInfo = new char[size];\n// Remember to delete versionInfo when it is no longer needed\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not require any specific library dependencies.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
