            {
              "id": "6a2ae470-ffd5-3e26-9dca-b2e861df4b8a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding highlights a genuine risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlapping memory regions in a memory copy operation. The analysis detected that the source and destination addresses may overlap, as indicated by data flow analysis. When using functions like memcpy, overlapping memory regions can result in unpredictable behavior, including data corruption. The source and destination expressions are both pointer arithmetic based on potentially related base pointers, and there is no evidence of explicit checks or safeguards to prevent overlap. Additionally, the source pointer could be null, which further increases the risk of undefined behavior. No explicit buffer size or guard is present to mitigate these risks. This is a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be addressed to ensure memory safety.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, replace the use of `memcpy` with `memmove`, which is designed to safely handle overlapping regions. This change ensures that the copy operation is performed correctly even if the source and destination addresses overlap.\n\n**Remediation Example:**\n\n```cpp\nstd::memmove((reinterpret_cast<address>(hostPtr) + dstOffs), (reinterpret_cast<const_address>(src) + srcOffs), size[0] * elementSize_);\n```\n\nAdditionally, consider adding explicit checks to ensure that neither the source nor the destination pointers are null before performing the memory operation to avoid null pointer dereference.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0f900ae5-56b2-3e34-816f-cb296692ef24",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible undefined behavior risk due to overlapping memory regions between the source and destination in a memory copy operation. The destination and source are both pointers, and dataflow analysis suggests that they may refer to overlapping memory. The use of `std::memcpy` in such a scenario is unsafe, as the C++ standard specifies that the behavior is undefined if the source and destination overlap. There are no explicit guards or checks in the code to prevent this overlap, and the function does not use a safer alternative that handles overlapping memory. Both the source and destination are confirmed to be non-null at this point, so the primary risk is the potential for memory corruption or unpredictable behavior due to overlap.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, replace `std::memcpy` with `std::memmove`, which is designed to safely handle overlapping memory. This change ensures that the copy operation is performed correctly even if the regions overlap.\n\n```cpp\nstd::memmove(hostPtr, src, copySize);\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e497b474-17bd-334a-8b8f-b9373a002b6f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible risk of undefined behavior due to overlapping memory regions between the source and destination in a memory copy operation. The dataflow analysis indicates that the destination buffer may overlap with the source buffer, which can cause undefined behavior when using functions like memcpy. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this overlap, and both the source and destination are pointers, making this risk more credible. The source and destination are not proven to be non-overlapping, and the function does not use a safer alternative that handles overlap. The source and destination are also not null, so the only concern is the overlap risk.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. `memmove` is designed to safely handle overlapping memory areas.\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(descCached, desc, sizeof(Resource::Descriptor));\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the copy operation is safe even if `descCached` and `desc` overlap in memory.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\n\n___\n"
