            {
              "id": "ec10e692-3613-3269-b183-552da177b67b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Found\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security issues. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards.\n\nIn the provided code, the `printf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string contains user-supplied data. This can allow an attacker to read or write arbitrary memory, cause a crash, or execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using unsafe C functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ standard library. For format string vulnerabilities specifically, ensure that format strings do not contain user-supplied data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `printf` function with a safer alternative. In this case, you could use `std::cout` with stream manipulators to control the format:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <iomanip>\n\n// ...\n\nstd::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(2) << *(reinterpret_cast<const double*>(argument)) << std::endl;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe original code appears to be using a custom `printf` function provided by the `amd::Os` namespace, so it would require the appropriate AMD library. The fixed code requires the `<iostream>` and `<iomanip>` libraries from the C++ standard library.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9e509ce1-de9e-381f-ac81-a225dffb30fd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure or unsafe C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In the provided code snippet, the `printf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not controlled by the programmer or if user-supplied data is used as a format string.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. For instance, instead of using `printf`, consider using `cout` or `fprintf` with a constant format string. Avoid using functions that do not check bounds or do not perform type checking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <cstdint>\n\nstd::cout << out.data() << *(reinterpret_cast<const uint64_t*>(argument)) << std::endl;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to be using a custom namespace `amd::Os`. The `printf` function is part of the C Standard Library (`<cstdio>` in C++ or `<stdio.h>` in C). The `reinterpret_cast` is a part of the C++ core language and does not require any library. The `uint64_t` type is defined in the `<cstdint>` header (or `<stdint.h>` in C).\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "da1786e4-9b18-3226-992d-75d20b90b451",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Found\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, and integer overflows. In the provided code snippet, the `printf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not controlled by the programmer or if user-supplied data is used as a format string.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these insecure C functions. For instance, instead of using `printf`, consider using `cout` or `cerr` in C++. If `printf` must be used, ensure that the format string is not user-controlled and does not contain any user-supplied data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n\nif (size == 2) {\n    std::cout << *(reinterpret_cast<const uint16_t *>(argument));\n} else {\n    std::cout << *(reinterpret_cast<const uint32_t *>(argument));\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not appear to require any specific library dependencies beyond the standard C++ libraries.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b6072802-6f73-31ad-989a-1d07953af6c8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In the provided code snippet, the `printf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not controlled by the programmer or if it's influenced by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. For instance, instead of using `printf`, consider using `cout` or `cerr` in C++. Also, always validate and sanitize user input to prevent any malicious data from causing harm.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <cstdint>\n\nif (size == 2) {\n    std::cout << *(reinterpret_cast<const uint16_t *>(argument));\n} else {\n    std::cout << *(reinterpret_cast<const uint32_t *>(argument));\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the following libraries:\n\n- `<cstdint>` for `uint16_t` and `uint32_t`\n- `<iostream>` for `std::cout`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "008f9b7f-d8bc-35e9-97d3-785911ef8009",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Found\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential for causing buffer overflow errors. In C++, this often includes functions like `printf()`, `strcpy()`, `gets()`, etc. These functions do not perform bounds checking, and can therefore write past the end of the allocated space, leading to buffer overflows. This can cause program instability, data corruption, and potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `amd::Os::printf(fmt.data(), fArg)`, the `printf()` function is used, which can lead to this vulnerability if not used carefully.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions that include bounds checking. For example, instead of `printf()`, you can use `snprintf()`, which allows you to specify the maximum number of characters to be written to the output buffer, preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `printf()` function with `snprintf()`. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\nchar buffer[50];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), fmt.data(), fArg);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example provided does not explicitly show any library dependencies. However, the `printf()` function is part of the C Standard Library, so `<cstdio>` or `<stdio.h>` would be required. If `fmt` is a `std::string`, `<string>` would also be required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d4580a44-a51a-3eb0-8c82-170413bcec05",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `printf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not controlled by the programmer or if user-supplied data is used as a format string.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. For instance, instead of `printf`, you can use `cout` or `fprintf` with a constant format string. Avoid using functions that do not check bounds or that may result in undefined behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `printf` function with a safer alternative. Here is a possible fix using `cout`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n\nstd::cout << hhFmt.data() << *(reinterpret_cast<const unsigned char*>(argument)) << std::endl;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `<iostream>` for `std::cout`.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "62187ec3-e5b2-3b00-b66b-c7d232ce07cd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure or unsafe C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflow, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflow. In the provided code snippet, the function `printf` is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if not used properly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. For instance, instead of using `printf`, you can use `fprintf` or `snprintf` which are safer as they limit the amount of data that can be written to the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nchar buffer[256];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), hlFmt.data(), fArg);\namd::Os::printf(\"%s\", buffer);\n```\n\nIn this fix, `snprintf` is used to prevent buffer overflow by limiting the amount of data written to the buffer. Then, `printf` is used to print the formatted string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to be using a custom library `amd::Os`. Therefore, it is assumed that this library is required for the code to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are active and accessible for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "dcb367a0-6ba1-3d24-81ea-7c8c9a980b19",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, and integer overflows. In this case, the `printf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not controlled by the programmer or if user-supplied data is used as a format string.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. For instance, instead of using `printf`, you can use `std::cout` for output. If you need to format the string, you can use `std::format` (C++20) or `sprintf` with a fixed format string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `printf` function with `std::cout`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n\nint main() {\n    std::string fmtF = \"-nan\";\n    std::cout << fmtF << std::endl;\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<iostream>`: for `std::cout`\n- `<string>`: for `std::string`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cecc08f3-e177-3389-a324-5b63ac382b0f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `printf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not controlled by the programmer or if user-supplied data is used as a format string.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. For instance, instead of using `printf`, you can use `cout` or `fprintf` with a constant format string. Also, avoid using user-supplied data as a format string. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nstd::cout << \"-infinity\";\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<iostream>`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n- [CWE-134: Use of Externally-Controlled Format String](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/134.html)"
